WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.800
What is your brain doing when you’re doing nothing?

2
00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:13.600
Your brain is about 2% of your body’s weight, but uses about 20% of its energy.

3
00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:19.700
And starting a task barely changes that.

4
00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:26.600
Brain scanners in the 1990s showed something strange.

5
00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:33.700
When people stopped a task and rested, some regions became more active, not less.

6
00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:39.800
In 2001, Marcus Raichle called it the brain’s “default mode”.

7
00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:45.700
Its main hubs sit along the midline: medial prefrontal and posterior cingulate cortex.

8
00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:53.400
It’s the network behind your inner monologue: memories, plans, and thoughts about yourself and others.

9
00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:59.700
It’s how you plan a life and understand people.

10
00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:07.400
But it runs a lot. In one Harvard study, minds wandered in 46.9% of moments.

11
00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:13.700
And wandering minds were less happy.

12
00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:21.800
So what happens when you try to sit with your breath?

13
00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:27.600
In 2012, Wendy Hasenkamp scanned 14 meditators focusing on the breath.

14
00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:33.000
Each time they caught their mind wandering, they pressed a button.

15
00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:38.400
Around each press, the brain moved through four phases.

16
00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:43.700
Wandering. Noticing. Shifting. Staying.

17
00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:53.600
Every catch is one repetition. Like a curl at the gym, for attention.

18
00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:59.600
In 2011, Judson Brewer’s team compared 12 experienced meditators with 12 beginners.

19
00:01:59.900 --> 00:02:04.800
The meditators averaged over 10,000 hours of practice.

20
00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:10.200
Across three styles of meditation, their default mode hubs were quieter.

21
00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:15.700
And those hubs were more tightly linked to regions for self-monitoring and control, even at rest.

22
00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:21.800
One caution: this compared two groups of people.

23
00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:27.700
It can’t prove meditation caused the difference.

24
00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:34.600
Meditation doesn’t switch off the default mode.

25
00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:41.700
It may change your relationship with it: noticing sooner, and coming back.

26
00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:49.800
Your mind will wander. That’s the default.
Coming back is the practice.
